+## Open bounded peer finding: exact-domino shortcut
+
+On current source SHA-256
+`9117be8035b5244cf7d95855b08b38f2a9e13d5e5b9b7a8b708d95de758b6270`,
+one ordinary default lesson (seed 0, medium, sample indices 1/2/3 as demos,
+4 as query) admits the plausible wrong rule “keep only same-colour cardinal
+components of exactly two cells.” All three demos contain only singletons and
+dominoes, so this rival matches every protected-Racket label. The query contains
+a triomino, which the authority preserves and the rival erases: three cells
+differ. All four inputs pass `self_check`; `lesson_identifiable` returns no
+reasons because this rival is absent from its six declared competitors.
+
+Executable reproduction and exact grids/labels are in packet evidence
+`checks/8e8d21720efb/probe.py` and `peer-result.json`, with completed command
+receipt `peer-command.json`. Executed canonical program SHA-256 is
+`89d99422f73ed0261300be3f62a00ca29212b923d5111eb1d5d0bd8eaf769646`
+(declared rule hash `76b667ae03a8`). This is an additional source-bound
+learnability limit, not a label defect, source repair, approval change, or
+resolution of earlier independent audits. Scope is one lesson and one rival;
+prevalence and hidden solve accuracy remain unmeasured. A future curriculum
+repair could put a size-3 surviving component in a retained demo for each query
+rotation, then explicitly check this rival.
